The b1nix archive key
=====================

/etc/apt/sources.list.d/b1nix.sources points at /etc/apt/keyrings/b1nix-archive.asc
as the key that signs the overlay's Release file.

This package creates the directory but does not ship the key: the key is a
release artifact, published on the website with its fingerprint, and shipping
it inside a package signed by itself proves nothing. The installer places it,
and anyone adding the repository by hand fetches it and checks the fingerprint
against the website before trusting it.

If the key is missing, apt refuses the overlay and says so. That is the correct
behaviour: an unsigned archive is not a safe fallback.
